lifeos-nix patch f-fabric-stdin-precedence — an explicit input source beats an inherited stdin

PROBLEM
  fabric drains fd 0 to EOF whenever stdin is not a character device, even when the
  command line already said where the input comes from (-y YouTube, -u scrape URL,
  -q scrape question). internal/cli/flags.go:

      info, _ := os.Stdin.Stat()
      pipedToStdin := (info.Mode() & os.ModeCharDevice) == 0
      ...
      if pipedToStdin { readStdin() }   // loops until EOF

  A caller that leaves stdin open therefore blocks forever with ZERO bytes on stdout
  AND stderr, and no error to explain it. Measured 2026-09-10:

      stdin = open pipe, -y URL   ->  killed at timeout, 0 bytes
      stdin = /dev/null, -y URL   ->  exit 0, 14282 bytes of transcript
      stdin = pty,       -y URL   ->  exit 0, 14282 bytes of transcript

RCA
  A tty IS a character device, so pipedToStdin is false and the branch is skipped for
  a human at a terminal. That is why upstream has not hit this: the defect is invisible
  to interactive use and exclusive to programmatic callers, which is what LifeOS is.

FIX SCOPE
  One condition. stdin is consulted only when no explicit source was given, so an
  explicit source wins and nothing else changes. Deliberately NOT fixed at the call
  sites: LifeOS documents bare `fabric -y URL` in six skills (Research, ExtractWisdom,
  Fabric, Aphorisms, Prompting, LIFEOS/TOOLS/GetTranscript.ts), so a per-caller
  `stdio: ignore` fixes only the callers someone thought to look at. A `< /dev/null`
  wrapper around the binary was rejected outright: it would amputate fabric's primary
  interface (`cat notes.md | fabric -p summarize`).

  vendorHash untouched on purpose — the hunk edits one file under internal/ and neither
  go.mod nor go.sum, so `go mod vendor` output is byte-identical. Note that nixpkgs'
  buildGoModule DOES inherit `patches` into the goModules FOD, so a future hunk here
  that touches either file will need the hash deliberately falsified and re-captured.

VERIFICATION
  Built and probed on BOTH resolutions, because they differ: this flake's own pin gives
  fabric-ai 1.4.453, while a consumer whose /etc/nixos sets
  `lifeos.inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs"` gets whatever its channel holds (1.4.459
  on the machine this was written on). The patch applies and builds on both.

  On 1.4.459, one variable changed, both directions measured:

      patched,   -y URL, stdin = open pipe  ->  exit 0,   14282 bytes
      unpatched, -y URL, stdin = open pipe  ->  exit 124,     0 bytes
      patched,   printf MARKER | fabric --dry-run  ->  MARKER still read

  The third line is the one that can sink this patch: with no source flag the filter
  interface must keep working. Automated guard: tests/fabric-test.sh asserts the patch
  is on the derivation (and that the .env tmpfiles verb stays `f`, never `f+`).

--- a/internal/cli/flags.go	2026-09-10 14:40:06.803044955 +0200
+++ b/internal/cli/flags.go	2026-09-10 14:40:17.316817182 +0200
@@ -229,7 +229,12 @@
 		ret.Message = AppendMessage(ret.Message, strings.Join(args, " "))
 	}
 
-	if pipedToStdin {
+	// An explicit input source on the command line beats an inherited stdin. -y/-u/-q
+	// already say where the input comes from, so draining fd 0 to EOF here can only
+	// block. Interactive use never hits this, because a tty is a character device and
+	// pipedToStdin is then false; a programmatic caller that leaves stdin open hangs
+	// forever with zero bytes on both stdout and stderr. Patched by lifeos-nix.
+	if pipedToStdin && ret.YouTube == "" && ret.ScrapeURL == "" && ret.ScrapeQuestion == "" {
 		var pipedMessage string
 		if pipedMessage, err = readStdin(); err != nil {
 			return
